showing how New Yorkers see the country.

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:38.240
I grew up in rural Oregon

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:40.770
and so this map sort of resonated with me.

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:43.510
I felt like New York had
this outsized presence

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:45.820
in our country, that everyone thought that

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:48.250
the United States was New York,

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:51.750
like it had some sort of
huge poll on everyone's mind.

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:53.770
And when I say huge, I don't mean

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:55.430
the eight and a half million people

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:57.400
that are packed into this city.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:01.544
I also mean the outsized
cultural gravity of this city.

00:01:01.544 --> 00:01:03.550
♪ New York, New York ♪

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:06.260
- It seems like every other
movie I watch, or book I read

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:09.480
is somehow connected to New York City.

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:12.500
So as we biked around this
city and had socially distanced

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:14.760
hangouts with our friends, and as we ate

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:17.170
the delicious things
this city has to offer,

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:19.170
I started to go down this rabbit hole

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:23.690
of how this city came to be
and how it became so damn big.

00:01:23.690 --> 00:01:27.300
- [Man] This is New York,
the world's largest city.

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:29.280
- So I've boiled it down to three reasons,

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.430
the three reasons why New York
City is so big and important.

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:35.228
Number one, water.

00:01:35.228 --> 00:01:37.480
(moderate music)

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:39.150
Let's look at a map real quick because,

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:41.560
you know, I like to look at maps.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:44.340
The growth of New York
City is primarily a story

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:47.290
of perfect access to water.

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:50.230
Look at where it is compared
to the Atlantic Ocean.

00:01:50.230 --> 00:01:53.580
It's not right on the ocean,
like it's not a beach,

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:58.030
you've got this nice long
island called Long Island

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:01.720
protecting it from the
elements and from invaders.

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:04.790
Zoom in and you see that
Staten Island is also here

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:07.560
to protect this city
from the wrath of the sea

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.130
or from conquers, and yet Manhattan

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:12.650
is just this tiny skip away from the ocean

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:15.750
for any boat fairing person
who wants to come and go.

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:19.680
Plus, these waters are super
deep, creating nice deep ports

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:21.550
which don't freeze during the winter.

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:23.290
They allow for loads of traffic

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:25.070
from bigger and bigger boats.

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:28.130
The first Europeans to
arrive and settle this land

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:30.610
were the Dutch, who were no strangers

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:33.680
to the economic advantages
of controlling water.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:35.770
In fact, I made a whole video about how

00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:37.920
the Dutch are obsessed
with controlling water.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:39.790
So anyway, the Dutch arrive and they see

00:02:39.790 --> 00:02:41.937
this prime piece of land and they're like,

00:02:41.937 --> 00:02:44.247
"Yep, this is a sweet piece of land.

00:02:44.247 --> 00:02:46.117
"We need this piece of land, let's name it

00:02:46.117 --> 00:02:47.627
"after our favorite city back home

00:02:47.627 --> 00:02:50.590
"that also has amazing access to water."

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:52.587
And New Amsterdam was born.

00:02:52.587 --> 00:02:54.400
(moderate music)

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:56.200
The Dutch taking over this piece of land

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.680
is really the beginning of the
modern history of New York.

