00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:14.748
What are the Dutch up to
on the island of Manhattan.

00:04:14.748 --> 00:04:16.520
(light music)

00:04:16.520 --> 00:04:18.880
As always, there were
people living on this land

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:20.430
who were not really familiar with

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:22.820
the concept of owning plots of land,

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:25.240
which clearly belonged to the earth dah!

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:26.667
And so the Dutch arrive and they're like,

00:04:26.667 --> 00:04:29.677
"Oh, so you don't mind if
we buy this land from you

00:04:29.677 --> 00:04:31.600
"and exploit it to get super rich?"

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:34.400
And so the Dutch paid the natives $24

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:37.610
and now the Dutch begin to
control this little plot of land

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:40.090
that is so perfectly connected to

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:42.063
and protected from the water.

00:04:42.063 --> 00:04:43.030
(light music)

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:46.010
They kept the original name
that the Algonquian people

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:48.383
had given this island
which was Manna-hata.

00:04:49.236 --> 00:04:51.160
And then they named
their surrounding areas

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:54.960
after Dutch names like Brooklyn and Bronx.

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:57.790
The British eventually took
over this piece of land from

00:04:57.790 --> 00:05:01.040
the Dutch and renamed it
after one of their own cities,

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:03.240
that had nothing to do with water,

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:05.680
and after a few years
of settling this land,

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:07.770
the British really started
leveling up when it came

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:09.163
to controlling the water.

00:05:10.390 --> 00:05:11.510
They started navigating up

00:05:11.510 --> 00:05:13.237
this river called the Hudson River.

00:05:13.237 --> 00:05:14.640
(moderate music)

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:17.000
So that they could connect
with the newly constructed

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:20.457
Erie Canal, which went all
the way to The Great Lakes.

00:05:20.457 --> 00:05:21.780
(moderate music)

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:23.470
And so now you have this one city

00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:26.100
that has protected access
to the Atlantic Ocean

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:29.433
as well as to the Great
Lakes all through water.

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:34.660
Okay, so perfect access
to water is number one

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:38.630
and the main reason why New
York City became so huge.

00:05:38.630 --> 00:05:42.107
Number two is that New York
became the world's factory.

00:05:42.107 --> 00:05:45.365
(light music)

00:05:45.365 --> 00:05:48.100
So it's the 1800s, and
there are three industries

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:49.940
that are really taking over the world.

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:52.460
Number one is the mass
production of clothing.

00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:54.570
Number two is the publishing of books

00:05:54.570 --> 00:05:56.780
since there's all this
sciency stuff going on

00:05:56.780 --> 00:05:59.060
and people wanna write
it down and publish it,

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:01.110
share it and everyone
wants to get really smart.

00:06:01.110 --> 00:06:04.030
And number three was
the refining of sugar,

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:06.060
which European powers were stealing

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:08.840
from all over the world to
feed the growing obsession

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:09.890
with sugar back home.

00:06:09.890 --> 00:06:12.000
These three have something in common,

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:15.640
they require some raw material
to be shipped in somewhere

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:19.390
to be transformed or refined or rearranged

00:06:19.390 --> 00:06:21.900
and then shipped out as a final product

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:23.530
to customers all over the world.

00:06:23.530 --> 00:06:26.220
To us in the 21st century
this seems super obvious

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:28.640
that our products would
travel around the world

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:30.380
to be transformed and manufactured

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:31.930
before they end up with us.

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:35.910
But back in the 1800s, a global
interconnected supply chain

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:38.100
was sort of a newish
thing, and New York City

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:41.330
was uniquely placed to take
advantage of this new process.

00:06:41.330 --> 00:06:43.370
To add to this momentum back in Britain,

00:06:43.370 --> 00:06:45.330
they were kinda pissed
off that their settlers

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:47.020
had declared independence from them,

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:48.360
and then had beat them in a war.

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:51.450
So as a matter of pride,
they sort of refused to trade

00:06:51.450 --> 00:06:53.670
with any of the major revolutionary cities

00:06:53.670 --> 00:06:55.550
like Philadelphia or Boston.

00:06:55.550 --> 00:06:57.780
So this meant that New
York City was the place

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:02.111
to do global business, and the
city exploded with industry.

00:07:02.111 --> 00:07:04.611
(light music)

00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:14.130
This boom in factories
and industrial production

00:07:14.130 --> 00:07:17.510
is really what built this
city, from the actual buildings

00:07:17.510 --> 00:07:19.730
and infrastructure, to the population,

00:07:19.730 --> 00:07:22.430
as workers flocked to the city for jobs.

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:24.080
Which gets me to my next point.

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:27.070
The third and potentially
most important element

00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:28.560
that built the New York City

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:31.013
that we know today, is immigration.

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:35.320
As this city became the
industrial hub for making stuff

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:37.740
for people around the world, a steady flow

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:40.290
of immigrants started
flowing into New York City,

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:41.770
mainly from Europe at first.

00:07:41.770 --> 00:07:45.370
Some of them were fleeing war
or oppression, starvation,

00:07:45.370 --> 00:07:47.270
and some were just looking for a new life,

00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:49.400
to take a risk, to try something new.

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:51.080
And New York was there to meet them.

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:53.050
So millions of immigrants started to flow

00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:55.610
into New York City, many getting on ships

00:07:55.610 --> 00:07:57.500
and arriving on this island.

00:07:57.500 --> 00:08:02.450
By 1860, almost 70% of adults in New York

00:08:02.450 --> 00:08:05.057
were foreign born, 70%.

00:08:05.057 --> 00:08:07.310
(upbeat music)

00:08:07.310 --> 00:08:10.060
These immigrants didn't just fill the jobs

00:08:10.060 --> 00:08:12.950
of the industrial and commercial
growth of New York City.

00:08:12.950 --> 00:08:16.360
This influx of immigrants from
so many different backgrounds

00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:20.110
began to lay the foundation
of the incredibly diverse

00:08:20.110 --> 00:08:23.890
multicultural experience
that is New York City today.

00:08:23.890 --> 00:08:27.070
(upbeat music)

00:08:27.070 --> 00:08:29.340
But even though they all came
from different backgrounds,

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:31.210
they also had one thing in common.

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:34.900
Immigrants tend to be a self
selecting group of risk takers.

00:08:34.900 --> 00:08:37.510
After all, they made
the long arduous journey

00:08:37.510 --> 00:08:39.720
into the unknown to start a new life.

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:42.690
In other words, they're
natural entrepreneurs.

00:08:42.690 --> 00:08:43.640
Look what we found.

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:46.803
Best tacos on the East Coast.

00:08:48.140 --> 00:08:50.710
Coz they just like Tijuana, here we go.

00:08:50.710 --> 00:08:52.901
(murmurs)

00:08:52.901 --> 00:08:55.040
- Now let's go to that park.

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:55.873
- [Woman] Num-num?

00:08:55.873 --> 00:08:57.500
- Yeah.
- So you take a bunch

00:08:57.500 --> 00:08:59.360
of risk takers and you gather them

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:02.980
into one large growing
city to build a new life,

00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:04.890
and that creates a culture of its own,

00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:08.070
a culture of new ideas, new
businesses, new inventions.

00:09:08.070 --> 00:09:09.740
And when you have a
bunch of new businesses

00:09:09.740 --> 00:09:11.520
and new inventions and new ideas,

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:13.870
you often have rich people show up

00:09:13.870 --> 00:09:15.610
to try to bet on those ideas

00:09:15.610 --> 00:09:18.860
to see whose idea will actually
win, with the intention

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:22.370
of making money on a
successful idea or invention.

00:09:22.370 --> 00:09:24.170
I'm using a bunch of words to describe

00:09:24.170 --> 00:09:26.900
the process of investing or finance.

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:29.410
For many years in the
United States, the epicenter

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:32.060
for banks and lenders was Philadelphia.

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:33.760
But as New York City filled up

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:36.520
with entrepreneurial
immigrants, the epicenter

00:09:36.520 --> 00:09:39.200
of investing slowly
shifted to the place where

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:40.580
the best bets were happening.

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:43.960
And New York City became
the financial epicenter.

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:46.590
All of the industrial
production and factories

00:09:46.590 --> 00:09:50.060
eventually went away, but the
banks and the stock exchange

00:09:50.060 --> 00:09:52.410
that were built on its shoulders stayed

00:09:52.410 --> 00:09:55.695
and became the economic
foundation of modern day New York.

00:09:55.695 --> 00:09:58.040
(moderate music)

00:09:58.040 --> 00:10:01.460
This explanation of New York
City helps me understand

00:10:01.460 --> 00:10:04.503
the more elusive mystique
about this place.

00:10:05.510 --> 00:10:07.400
And that it's poll isn't just about

00:10:07.400 --> 00:10:09.810
the eight and a half million
people that live here,

00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:12.330
but that those many people, their stories,

00:10:12.330 --> 00:10:14.680
where they came from, the
lives they built here,

00:10:14.680 --> 00:10:16.220
all from different backgrounds,

00:10:16.220 --> 00:10:19.200
and yet they were all engaged
in the same experiment,

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:21.510
cramming into this city to build something

00:10:21.510 --> 00:10:23.670
that had never been built before.

00:10:23.670 --> 00:10:26.250
- [Presenter 1] New York City
is as amazing in its own way

00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:27.760
as the Grand Canyon.

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:29.210
- [Presenter 2] New York City.
- [Presenter 3] New York City.

00:10:29.210 --> 00:10:30.710
- [Presenter 4] New York City.

00:10:30.710 --> 00:10:33.480
- So there you have it,
New York City is a place

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:35.970
that had a perfect
connection to the water,

00:10:35.970 --> 00:10:38.510
that was perfectly placed
to be the world's factory,

00:10:38.510 --> 00:10:40.720
as the global economy started to blossom.

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:43.720
All of this attracting
risk taking immigrants,

00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:46.930
who built the population
but also planted the seeds

00:10:46.930 --> 00:10:50.094
for the uniquely New York
character that we know today.

00:10:50.094 --> 00:10:52.677
(upbeat music)

